WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.730
On Friday my class visits a farm in Dala.

2
00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:06.090
I want an agent to check the

3
00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:08.110
road and the weather, and remind

4
00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:09.290
me to tell the parents.

5
00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:11.540
Will it do them in the right order?

6
00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.240
Today: handing an agent a task in steps.

7
00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:20.270
Most school jobs take more than one

8
00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:23.590
step: find something, then act on it.

9
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.380
An agent plans the steps, then

10
00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:28.260
calls one tool after another.

11
00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:32.540
A plan is the list of steps an agent will

12
00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:35.200
take, in order, with a tool for each.

13
00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:38.090
A good plan also says which

14
00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:39.790
step needs which result.

15
00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.140
Darsi Tiny writes its plan in one line.

16
00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:47.790
Here’s Darsi Tiny with a two-step

17
00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:51.090
goal: six B’s first lesson tomorrow,

18
00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:53.550
and a reminder at seven thirty.

19
00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:57.470
First the timetable, which answers P E.

20
00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:00.240
Then the reminder, written from

21
00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.900
that result: six B, P E.

22
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.240
Okafor’s trip has the same shape.

23
00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:08.630
The route tool says Kafi to

24
00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:10.270
Dala is seventy kilometres.

25
00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:12.880
Then the weather tool checks Dala:

26
00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:15.740
partly cloudy, a high of thirty-two.

27
00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:17.500
Quick check!

28
00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:22.410
On Saturday there’s no school, so the

29
00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:25.070
timetable answers: no school.

30
00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:28.300
What does Darsi Tiny do next?

31
00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.980
It still sets the reminder, six

32
00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:33.980
B, six B, for Saturday.

33
00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:36.870
It never checked the timetable’s

34
00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.670
answer before using it.

35
00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:41.350
Big models can make the same slip.

36
00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:45.050
So the check after each step must be

37
00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:47.390
built in, by the app or by you.

38
00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.060
So plan the checks too.

39
00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:52.060
After a step that finds

40
00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:53.500
something, look at the result.

41
00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:56.950
Before a step that acts, like a

42
00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.410
reminder or a message, the app

43
00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:01.270
should stop for your yes.

44
00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:04.530
Many teacher jobs fit this shape.

45
00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.380
Find who was absent this week, then

46
00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:08.900
draft a note for each family.

47
00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:11.220
Look up a word, then find

48
00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:12.540
library books about it.

49
00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:14.470
Check tomorrow’s first

50
00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:16.590
lesson, then set a reminder.

51
00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:19.790
In the app, pick a goal and

52
00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:21.490
guess its tools, in order.

53
00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:24.640
Then compare your plan with Darsi Tiny’s.

54
00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:27.140
Coloured chips show where each

55
00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:28.520
word of a call came from.

56
00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:29.900
And try Saturday.

57
00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:31.930
Your turn.

58
00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:35.940
In the reminder six B, P E, where

59
00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:37.540
did the words P E come from?

60
00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:40.380
From the timetable’s result.

61
00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:43.750
Remember: a job is a chain of

62
00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:45.450
steps, each with a tool.

63
00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.500
One step’s result can feed the next.

64
00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.780
And a failed step can still lead to an

65
00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:54.380
action, so check after each step.

66
00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:57.830
Try it in class tomorrow: plan a

67
00:02:57.830 --> 00:02:59.350
class trip as tool steps.

68
00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:03.100
Groups write each step on a card: the

69
00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:05.700
tool, what it needs, and what it gives

70
00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:05.960
back.

71
00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:08.710
Then they mark where a person checks,

72
00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:11.230
and what happens if a step fails.
